A producer and a consumer share a circular buffer `item buffer[BUFFER_SIZE]` with `BUFFER_SIZE = 8`, using only the two indices `in` and `out` (initially 0):

```c
/* producer */
while ((in + 1) % BUFFER_SIZE == out) ; /* wait: full */
buffer[in] = next_produced;
in = (in + 1) % BUFFER_SIZE;

/* consumer */
while (in == out) ; /* wait: empty */
next_consumed = buffer[out];
out = (out + 1) % BUFFER_SIZE;
```

What is the maximum number of items that can be in the buffer at the same time?

Consider the following program (error checks omitted; every `fork()` succeeds).

```c
int id = shmget(IPC_PRIVATE, sizeof(int), IPC_CREAT | 0600);
int *s = (int *) shmat(id, NULL, 0);
int g = 1;
*s = 1;
if (fork() == 0) {                 /* C: child */
    *s = *s + 10;
    g = g + 10;
    shmdt(s);                      /* C detaches this attachment */
    int *t = (int *) shmat(id, NULL, 0);
    if (fork() == 0) {             /* G: child of C */
        *t = *t * 2;
        g = g * 2;
        exit(0);
    }
    wait(NULL);
    *t = *t + g;
    exit(0);
}
wait(NULL);
printf("%d %d\n", *s, g);
```

What does the original (parent) process print?

A circular buffer of `N ≥ 3` slots is shared through shared memory. The reference code is:

```c
/* producer */                      /* consumer */
while ((in + 1) % N == out) ;       while (in == out) ;
buffer[in] = item;                  item = buffer[out];
in = (in + 1) % N;                  out = (out + 1) % N;
```

`in` and `out` start at 0. Assume sequentially consistent memory, that each individual load or store of `in`, `out` or a buffer slot is atomic, and that statements of different processes may interleave arbitrarily at that granularity. No locks are used. Which statements are **correct**?

Two processes use direct communication with **blocking send** and **blocking receive**:

```text
P1:  send(P2, a);   receive(P2, &b);
P2:  send(P1, c);   receive(P1, &d);
```

For which link capacities is completion of both processes **guaranteed**, whatever the scheduling?

Processes P and Q communicate through two mailboxes, M1 (used only P → Q) and M2 (used only Q → P). Both mailboxes have the **same capacity k** messages; `send` blocks while the mailbox is full and `receive` blocks while it is empty.

```text
P:  repeat 6 times: send(M1, x);
    repeat 6 times: receive(M2, &y);

Q:  repeat 2 times: receive(M1, &x);
    repeat 6 times: send(M2, y);
    repeat 4 times: receive(M1, &x);
```

What is the **minimum** k for which both processes are guaranteed to terminate under every possible scheduling?

```c
int fd[2]; char c;
pipe(fd);
if (fork() == 0) {                     /* child */
    while (read(fd[0], &c, 1) > 0)
        write(STDOUT_FILENO, &c, 1);   /* unbuffered */
    printf("\ndone\n");
    exit(0);
}
close(fd[0]);                          /* parent */
write(fd[1], "hi", 2);
close(fd[1]);
wait(NULL);
```

What happens when this program runs?

```c
int fd[2]; char buf[10];
pipe(fd);
if (fork() == 0) {
    write(fd[1], "123", 3);
    write(fd[1], "45", 2);
    exit(0);
}
wait(NULL);
int n = read(fd[0], buf, 10);
printf("%d\n", n);
```

What value does the parent print? (Assume all calls succeed.)

A programmer tries to implement a ping–pong exchange with **one** pipe:

```c
int fd[2]; char b[5] = {0};
pipe(fd);
if (fork() == 0) {                 /* child */
    read(fd[0], b, 4);
    write(fd[1], "pong", 4);
    exit(0);
}
write(fd[1], "ping", 4);           /* parent */
read(fd[0], b, 4);
printf("%s\n", b);
```

Which statement is correct?

```c
int fd[2], n = 0; char c;
pid_t root = getpid();
pipe(fd);
for (int i = 0; i < 2; i++) {
    if (fork() || fork())
        write(fd[1], "x", 1);
    else
        close(fd[1]);
}
if (getpid() != root)
    exit(0);
close(fd[1]);
while (read(fd[0], &c, 1) > 0)
    n++;
printf("%d\n", n);
```

All `fork()` calls succeed. A `write` on a descriptor that the process has already closed fails and writes nothing (the same for a second `close`). What number does the original process print?

```c
int p[2];
pipe(p);
if (fork() == 0) {                    /* W: writer */
    close(p[0]);
    if (fork() == 0) {                /* G: child of W */
        sleep(10);
        exit(0);
    }
    write(p[1], "abc", 3);
    exit(0);
}
if (fork() == 0) {                    /* R: reader */
    close(p[1]);
    int n = 0; char c;
    while (read(p[0], &c, 1) > 0) n++;
    printf("%d\n", n);
    exit(0);
}
close(p[0]);                          /* parent */
wait(NULL);
wait(NULL);
close(p[1]);
```

All calls succeed and nothing else touches the pipe. Which statements are **correct**?

```c
int fd[2]; char buf[64]; int n;
pipe(fd);
if (fork() == 0) {
    dup2(fd[1], STDOUT_FILENO);
    close(fd[0]); close(fd[1]);
    printf("hi\n");
    execlp("echo", "echo", "bye", (char *) NULL);
    exit(1);
}
close(fd[1]);
while ((n = read(fd[0], buf, sizeof buf)) > 0)
    write(STDOUT_FILENO, buf, n);   /* parent's stdout is the terminal */
wait(NULL);
```

Assume standard C stdio rules: `stdout` is line-buffered when it is a terminal and **fully buffered** otherwise. What appears on the terminal?

Processes P and Q communicate through two mailboxes: M1 (used only P → Q) with capacity **x** and M2 (used only Q → P) with capacity **y**, where x and y are non-negative integers. `send` blocks while its mailbox is full; capacity 0 means a **rendezvous** (`send` blocks until the receiver executes the matching `receive`). `receive` blocks while the mailbox is empty.

```text
P:  repeat 5 times: send(M1, a);
    repeat 5 times: receive(M2, &b);

Q:  repeat 3 times: send(M2, b);
    repeat 5 times: receive(M1, &a);
    repeat 2 times: send(M2, b);
```

What is the **minimum value of x + y** for which both processes are guaranteed to terminate under every scheduling?

Three processes communicate in a ring through mailboxes M1 (P → Q), M2 (Q → R) and M3 (R → P). Every mailbox has the **same capacity k ≥ 0** (k = 0 means rendezvous); `send` blocks while its mailbox is full and `receive` blocks while it is empty.

```text
P:  4 × send(M1);  2 × receive(M3);  2 × send(M1);  2 × receive(M3);
Q:  1 × receive(M1);  4 × send(M2);  5 × receive(M1);
R:  2 × receive(M2);  4 × send(M3);  2 × receive(M2);
```

What is the **minimum k** for which all three processes are guaranteed to terminate under every scheduling?
